			<div class="content" id="post-content-1521376">
									

<p>Yeah. People forget the size of the moon sometimes. The equatorial circumference is 10,921&#160;km, which means that a trip from one side to the other would not just be a casual Sunday drive.</p>
<p>Still trying to make up my mind on this one. I'm hovering somewhere between 0 and +1, with the images being the biggest sticking point. Decently done article, though.</p>
<p>A thought on improving the image: if you can get a nice, big high quality LRO image of the far side of the moon, that image itself will probably be a composite image of several smaller photographs. Perhaps cut that image up into its individual sub-images, modify each of those, and then re-assemble? Having a consistent-looking overlaid image on a composite image (which appears to be what you have right now) isn't going to look as real as having the imperfections that would result from having it assembled from parts. Just a thought - free advice worth the cost.</p>

							</div>

								
					
